  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1222 AM MDT Thu Jul 30 2026/ \n\n Upper level ridging through the day on Saturday will help to \nmaintain well above normal temperatures across all of the Northern \nRockies, with high temperatures peaking on Saturday as increasing \nflow and strong warm air advection brings readings into the mid-90s \nto near 110 degrees. While overall dry conditions will persist \nbeneath this ridge there remains enough monsoonal moisture spreading \nnorth and east across portions of Southwest and into Central Montana \nto support isolated to widely scattered showers and thunderstorms \nduring the afternoon and evening hours through Friday. Biggest \nimpact from the showers and thunderstorms will be gusty and erratic \noutflow winds. By Saturday night the upper level ridge will be \nbroken down by a potent upper level trough sliding east across \nSouthern Canada, with this trough and attendant (dry) cold front \nbrining gusty winds to all areas for the day on Sunday and below \ntemperatures for the day on Monday. - Moldan\n\n- Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nReturn of Extreme Temperatures by Saturday...\n\nClimate anomaly indicators remain honed in on Saturday being the \nhottest day of the upcoming week, with ECMWF EFI values peaking \nbetween 0.75 and 0.9 across Southwest through North Central Montana; \nwhich continues to suggest unusual to very unusual hot temperatures \nwith respect to early August climatology. This is remarkable \nconsidering that August 1st is only one day removed from the \nclimatological peak for average high temperatures across Southwest \nthrough North Central Montana. \n\nThe table below represents the potential high temperature scenarios \nfor select cities across Southwest through North Central Montana, \nwith the \"Low End\" scenario being the NBM10Pct (10% chance that \ntemperatures are lower than the listed value), \"Most Likely\" \nscenario being the NBM50Pct, and the \"High End\" scenario being the \nNBM90Pct (10% chance that temperatures exceed the listed value).\n\n                    Saturday's High Temperature Scenarios\nLOCATION               Low End | Most Likely | High End\nBrowning                  88   |      93     |     95\nCut Bank                  95   |     100     |    102\nHavre                    104   |     108     |    110\nGreat Falls              102   |     103     |    105\nLewistown                 98   |     101     |    103\nHelena                    98   |     100     |    104\nWhite Sulphur Springs     94   |      97     |     99       \nBozeman                   99   |     103     |    105\nDillon                    94   |      97     |     98\nEnnis                     93   |      97     |     99\nWest Yellowstone          89   |      91     |     93\n\nWhile wildfire smoke can significantly influence high temperatures \nby limit heating...the past few days have only seen minor impacts to \nforecasted highs. Therefore the earlier (in the week) concerns that \nI had on forecasted highs being held below the 50Pct, or \"Most \nLikely\" scenario, on Saturday have decreased. Given this increased \nconfidence in hot temperatures occurring on Saturday, most notably \nover portions of the Central and North Central Montana plains, an \nExtreme Heat Watch has been issued from 18z Saturday through 18z \nSunday due to the hot daytime temperatures and limited cooling (as \ncompared to climatology) during the overnight hours. \n\nHigh Winds from Saturday night through Sunday morning...\n\nH700 winds of 40-60kts, which is an impressive 3 to nearly 4 \nstandard deviations above normal per NAEFS analysis, are prog'd to \noverspread the Rocky Mountain Front from 00z Sunday through 18z \nSunday. BUFKIT soundings support ideal thermal profiles, especially \nbetween 06z and 14z Sunday, for mountain wave activity across the \nEastern Glacier Park Region out to Cut Bank. This anomalous cross \nbarrier flow combined with the potential for mountain wave activity \nhas increased concerns that High Winds could materialize across all \nof Glacier County and portions of western Toole and central Pondera \nCounties. For this reason a High Wind Watch has been issued from 03z \nSunday through 18z Sunday for these areas, with the most concerning \ntimeframe being between 06-14z Sunday. Those looking to travel or \nrecreate along the Rocky Mountain Front should be prepared for \nstrong winds; which could make driving and hiking difficult, lead to \nfalling trees in the backcountry, and/or lead to the spread of \nexisting/new wildfires.\n\nThe table below represents the potential wind gust scenarios for \nselect locations along the Rocky Mountain Front, with the \"Low End\" \nscenario being the NBM10Pct (10% chance that gusts are lower than \nthe listed value), \"Most Likely\" scenario being the NBM50Pct, and \nthe \"High End\" scenario being the NBM90Pct (10% chance that gusts \nexceed the listed value).\n\n                Saturday Night/Sunday Wind Gust Scenarios\nLOCATION               Low End | Most Likely | High End\nMany Glacier Valley    35 mph  |    50 mph   |  60 mph\nLog",
